.TH PSF-PHP2SHELL-WATCH 8 "30 October 2026" "psf-security-patches 26.10.30-1.0.0" "System Administration"
.ad l
.SH NAME
.ad l
psf-php2shell-watch \- php2shell-watch: follow PHP and web server logs live and flag trouble
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ad l
.B /\:\:opt/\:\:psf\-security\-patches/\:\:php/\:\:php2shell\-watch.sh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.ad l
.PP
CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619
.PP
Prints one line per hit, and a count summary on stop. A hit is a lead to
investigate: extensions crash workers too, and the upload-progress field is in
legit forms. Only patching PHP fixes the bug.
.PP
Design notes, from a 24 October 2026 honeypot report by the phpot project
(35 confirmed shell executions on single-worker Apache mod_php):
https:/\:/\:github.com/\:0x4D31/\:honeypot\-data/\:blob/\:main/\:reports/\:php2shell/\:2026\-10\-24\-confirmed\-rce.md
.RS 2
.nf
* The stages of that chain answered HTTP 200, and the final
  request, the
  one that starts the shell, never reached the access log because
    the worker
  died. The POST-500 correlation below cannot see that. The
    pre-script-OOM
  then same-worker-crash correlation and the per-client OOM burst
    can.
* The strongest signal in that report was process-level: an Apache
  worker
  starting /bin/sh. Logs only show the residue, so this script
    also polls
  /proc for shells and tools that appear under a web worker
    (PROCS=0 to
  turn off). It is a poller: a process that starts and exits
    between two
  samples is missed, and one whose worker has already died is
    re-parented
  and no longer links to it. For a guarantee use execve auditing
    (auditd,
  eBPF) instead; this is the cheap version.
* Request fingerprints and source IPs are weak, fast-aging
  signals: an
  attacker changes them. They are here as extra leads, not as
    protection.
* The q-parameter fingerprint matches both the short numbered form
  (?q0=v&q1=v&q2=v...) and the seven-digit form, because different
    clients
  used different ones. A process this script flags shows its TCP
    peers
  (the phpot report's shells tried to call out), and a POST with a
    very
  large response, or a PHPSESSID value thousands of characters
    long, is
  flagged as a LEAD: the disclosure stage returned a PHPSESSID of
    819,200
  bytes decoded (about 2 MB on the wire).
* The per-client burst and the same-worker chain alert need the
  error-log
  line to carry the worker PID and the client address. Apache's
    own error
  log does; PHP's own error_log file format ("[23-Oct-2026
    09:08:35 UTC]
  PHP Fatal error: ...") carries neither, so those two alerts
    cannot fire
  from it. The plain per-line signature still does. The first such
    line
  prints a one-time NOTE.
* nginx's "upstream sent too big header" error is flagged as a
  LEAD. At
  default fastcgi buffer sizes nginx refuses a leak-sized
    Set-Cookie with a
  502 and that line, so on nginx it is the counterpart of the
    oversized
  response size the access log cannot show. Large legitimate
    headers raise
  it too.
.fi
.RE
.SH COMMANDS AND OPTIONS
.ad l
.TP
\fBsudo ./php2shell\-watch.sh\fR
.ad l
# watch here; Ctrl-C to stop
.TP
\fBsudo nohup ./php2shell\-watch.sh >/var/log/php2shell\-watch.log 2>&1 &\fR
.ad l
# background
.TP
\fBtail \-f /var/log/php2shell\-watch.log # then watch it\fR
.ad l

.TP
\fBLOGS="/a.log /b.log" ./php2shell\-watch.sh\fR
.ad l
# watch specific files
.SH FILES
.ad l
.TP
\fB/\:\:opt/\:\:psf\-security\-patches/\:\:php/\:\:php2shell\-watch.sh\fR
.ad l
The script.
.SH SEE ALSO
.ad l
